prompt_template:
Create a {ASPECT_RATIO} finished editorial poster in the "prismatic-glass-animal-weekend-editorial" visual style.

Subject: {SUBJECT}
Action: {SUBJECT_ACTION}
Prop/Product: {PRODUCT_OR_PROP}
Location tag: {LOCATION}
Background and text zones: {BACKGROUND_ELEMENTS}
Main text: {MAIN_TEXT}
Secondary text: {SECONDARY_TEXT}
Accent symbol: {ACCENT_SYMBOL}
Sculpture styling: {WARDROBE_STYLE}
Style fidelity anchors: {STYLE_FIDELITY_ANCHORS}
Source content to avoid: {SOURCE_CONTENT_TO_AVOID}

Apply these literal style rules:
- Composition: use one oversized animal sculpture occupying about 65 to 80 percent of the canvas on a pure matte-black field. Arrange it in a dynamic three-quarter studio view, slightly off center, with a bold crop and at least one third of the image preserved as clean black negative space. Adapt the layout natively to the requested aspect ratio.
- Typography: typeset the exact supplied words cleanly in white. Use the animal name as a huge extended italic uppercase sans identity word; set the remaining headline as ultra-light geometric uppercase lines stacked in a side column; place tiny widely tracked issue microcopy in a corner; place the location tag above a compact two-line bold italic caption near the lower edge. Keep all text clear of busy reflections.
- Color: keep the field nearly pure black. Build the subject from transparent smoky charcoal glass, mirror-black liquid chrome, cool silver edges, and narrow prismatic caustics in cyan, cobalt, violet, magenta, coral orange, and warm white. Rainbow color must remain a restrained reflective accent, not a full multicolor fill.
- Texture and lighting: render pristine high-end CGI with thick blown-glass depth, smooth anatomy, crisp internal refraction, razor-sharp beveled highlights, and narrow studio rim lights. Preserve deep blacks and avoid ambient haze.
- Graphic elements: use only sparse typographic clusters, one tiny issue marker, short microcopy lines, and deliberate black gaps. Do not add boxes, frames, stickers, scenery, floor planes, or decorative illustrations.

Avoid: the original dog and its seated upward-looking pose; the name LEBRON and every source phrase; exact source line breaks or pixel layout; logos; celebrities; mascots; watermarks; usernames; creator credits; QR codes; app UI; scenic backgrounds; furniture sets; flat cartoons; plush toys; clay renders; dense collages; opaque rainbow plastic; noisy grain; glow haze; chromatic smearing; illegible or invented filler text.

Important content rule: match the style fidelity anchors first, then present the specified new animal and new weekend ritual. Keep the theme close to cute miniature weekend life while changing the exact subject, action, prop, wording, and story from the source.
